[Music]  delivery is picking up steam in the  United States analysts project a steady  increase in the share of delivery sales  at restaurants over the next several  years but what's convenient for  customers might not be best for the  restaurants part of the challenge is the  cost of working with third-party apps  which analysts say will be servicing  many of these orders but this trend is  hitting a nerve in the restaurant  business these bar charts can help  explain why this analysis compares meal  prices for cost for in-store service and  delivery by a third-party app when  restaurants serve food in their store  their margins are up to 69 percent of  the total check and the same meal  ordered via an app well that's the price  of convenience for you additional costs  in labor packing and fees to the apps  make the profit margin sink to thirty  nine percent of the total check on  average food sellers will pay  third-party services a fee of ten to 25  percent on each order vendors who  partner with third-party apps also lose  control over valuable customer data for  restaurants who want to bypass  third-party apps and still get into the  delivery game there are other costs  consider Panera's delivery business the  chain spent six years building it it  costs upwards of a hundred million  dollars to develop its online ordering  system that investment cut into profits  for three years but the effort began to  pay off in 2016 today each delivery  costs about five dollars after  accounting for labor gas and packaging  but to avoid turning away customers  Panera charges a flat delivery fee of  three dollars per order in most markets  this means that they have to sell a lot  more per order to absorb those costs  many smaller restaurants can't afford  that kind of investment order-taking  systems must be developed drivers have  to be hired a third-party app might take  care of those logistics but they take a  commission and typically customers  aren't keen to pay the difference  according to a survey by till stirred  85% of consumers aren't willing to pay  more than $5 for restaurant delivery and  customers may wind up unhappy anyway  because many restaurants sell food that  doesn't travel well this analysis from  Cohen &amp; Co reveals a rise in the share  of orders from steak houses and burger  chains delivery staples like pizza or  Indian foods are losing pace but the  businesses that previously dominated the  delivery market sold food that can stay  tasty for longer periods of time whereas  say french fries prepped in store  probably won't make it to your doorstep  tasting their best and when that order  of fries shows up cold consumers tend to  blame the food seller even if it was  delivered by a third-party app despite  the shortcomings the rise of delivery  seems inevitable though the field is  crowded third-party delivery platforms  are expected to rise in value over the  next few years which means that like it  or not many urban restaurants may soon  have to embrace a brave new world of  food delivery

